  <h2>Why Yangshuo?</h2>
  <p>Yangshuo is the real heart of the Guilin region. The town itself is a small, walkable riverside settlement of about 300,000 people. But the surrounding countryside — a maze of limestone karst peaks, bamboo groves, rice paddies, and the Yulong River — is one of the most dramatic landscapes on Earth. This is the landscape you see in Chinese paintings, on the 20 RMB banknote, and in every travel magazine. Yangshuo has been drawing travelers since the 1980s, when it was a backpacker secret. Today it's more developed but no less spectacular.</p>

  <h2>Getting Around</h2>
  <p>The best way to explore Yangshuo is by bicycle or e-bike. Rentals cost 30-50 RMB per day for a standard bike and 50-80 RMB for an e-bike from any shop on West Street or Pan Tao Road. No deposit needed — just leave your hotel name. The roads are flat and the scenery is constant. For longer trips to Xingping (25 km) or the Yulong River valley, a taxi or Didi costs 40-80 RMB. Local buses are available but infrequent and hard to navigate without Chinese.</p>

  <h2>Top Things to Do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Li River bamboo raft</strong> (Yangdi to Jiuma section, 1.5 hours, ~120 RMB) — the most scenic stretch of the river</li>
    <li><strong>Ten Mile Gallery bike ride</strong> — flat 10 km road flanked by karst peaks, with stops at the Yulong River bridge and Moon Hill</li>
    <li><strong>Hike Moon Hill</strong> (15 RMB entry, 30-40 min up) — 800 stone steps through a natural arch to a 360-degree panorama</li>
    <li><strong>Yulong River bamboo raft</strong> (150-200 RMB per raft, 2 people) — quieter than the Li River, past ancient stone bridges</li>
    <li><strong>Rock climbing</strong> — over 1,000 bolted routes on limestone crags within 30 minutes of town</li>
    <li><strong>Cooking class</strong> — learn to make beer fish and Guilin rice noodles (~150 RMB for a half-day class)</li>
    <li><strong>Impression Sanjie Liu light show</strong> — directed by Zhang Yimou (of Beijing Olympics opening ceremony fame), performed on the Li River at night (198 RMB)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where to Eat</h2>
  <p>West Street has the tourist restaurants with English menus and higher prices. For the real experience, walk two blocks into the side streets (Guihua Road, Diecui Road) and look for places busy with locals. Beer fish (60-100 RMB for two) is the regional specialty — freshwater fish cooked in beer with tomatoes and peppers. Rice noodles (8-15 RMB per bowl) are eaten for breakfast, lunch, and late-night snacks. For street food, try bamboo rice (sticky rice roasted in bamboo tubes) and grilled Li River fish on skewers.</p>

  <h2>Best Time to Visit</h2>
  <p>April-May and September-October — pleasant temperatures, manageable crowds. The landscape is photogenic year-round but each season has a different character. Spring is lush green with occasional rain. Summer is hot but the karst peaks wrapped in mist are incredibly atmospheric. Autumn has the best light and clearest skies. Winter is cold and quiet with few tourists — and if you're lucky, mist-shrouded peaks that look exactly like classical Chinese ink paintings.</p>

  <h2>How Long to Stay</h2>
  <p>Two nights minimum — one for the Li River and arrival, one for the countryside. Three nights is the sweet spot. Four or five nights lets you add Longji Rice Terraces and Xingping at a relaxed pace. Don't try to do Yangshuo as a day trip from Guilin — you'll spend half your time in transit and miss the morning and evening light that makes the landscape magical.</p>

